{
	title: 'Print mode: hal -p runs one prompt and prints the reply',
	spec: 'Like `claude -p`: `hal -p <prompt> [-d <dir>] [-m <model>]` (long forms --print, --dir, --model; options in any order; the prompt is the one argument that is not an option) opens a new tab in <dir> (default: the current directory; it must be an existing directory, else exit 2), switches its model as /model does when -m is given (aliases and :effort included), sends the prompt and waits for the turn to end. The turn is a full one, every tool included. The tab is an ordinary tab: every client sees it and the user may watch, steer, answer an approval or pause it. A completed turn prints its final text (what the model wrote after its last tool call) and a newline to stdout, exit 0. A refused model closes the tab again and, like a paused, failed or canceled turn, says why on stderr and exits 1. A question the turn asks is named on stderr (the turn waits for an answer in Hal). It joins the running host or, if none, hosts for its own duration without starting the web server or recovering other sessions turns.',
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		"connection.start resets pending commands, so print.run's begin() sends tab-new only after link.start resolves; earlier sends are silently lost (it hung). The /model reply and the prompt race: the prompt is submitted only after /model's output arrives, error or not, else a bad -m ran the prompt on the old model. Final text resets at each tool_call and drops the <summary> line (common/summary.ts). Hosting for its own run uses server.serve directly, skipping becomeHost (web, recovery, model refresh)."
	],
	needs: ['cb']
}
